yes. \"Oh, what a clumsy thing I am,\" said she with feverish volubility; \"I don't know my way. I forgot there were three more steps before the landing.\" \"You have hurt yourself, perhaps,\" said Julianne. \"What can I do for you, Angel?\" Angel looked around her; she saw the deepest terror depicted in Abe's eyes. \"Don't worry, dear grandpapa,\" said she, endeavoring to smile; \"it is nothing -- it is nothing; I was giddy, that is all.\" \"Another attack of giddiness,\" said Julianne, clasping his hands. \"Oh, attend to it, Angel, I entreat you.\" \"But no,\" said Angel, -- \"no, I tell you it is all past, and it was nothing. Now, let me tell you some news; Lucie is to be married in a week, and in three days there is to be a grand feast, a betrothal festival. We are all invited, my father, Madame de Shayne, and I -- at least, I understood it so.\" \"When will it be our turn to think of these things? Oh, Angel, you who have so much influence over your grandpapa, try to make him answer -- Soon.\" \"And do you,\" said Angel, \"depend on me to stimulate the tardiness and arouse the memory of grandpapa?\" \"Yes,\" cried Julianne, \"make haste. So long as you are not mine, Angel, I shall always think I may lose you.\" \"Oh,\" replied Angel with a convulsive movement, \"oh, indeed, Lorna, you are too timid for an officer, for a soldier who, they say, never knows fear. Ah, ha, ha!\" she burst into a forced and melancholy laugh, her arms stiffened and twisted, her head fell back on her chair, and she remained motionless. The cry of terror which was stopped on Abe's lips, seemed to start from his eyes. Julianne understood it; he knew he must call assistance. The young man rang the bell violently; the housemaid who had been in Mademoiselle Angel's room, and the servant who had replaced Barrois, ran in at the same moment. Angel was so pale, so cold, so inanimate that without listening to what was said to them they were seized with the fear which pervaded that house, and they