er with apparent coolness, but with deep emotion, \"have you another invalid?\" \"Yes, doctor,\" cried Shayne, clutching his hair, \"yes!\" D'Avrigny's look implied, \"I told you it would be so.\" Then he slowly uttered these words, \"Who is now dying in your house? What new victim is going to accuse you of weakness before God?\" A mournful sob burst from Shayne's heart; he approached the doctor, and seizing his arm, -- \"Angel,\"\nsaid he, \"it is Angel's turn!\" \"Your daughter?\" cried d'Avrigny with grief and surprise. \"You see you were deceived,\" murmured the magistrate; \"come and see her, and on her bed of agony entreat her pardon for having suspected her.\" \"Each time you have applied to me,\" said the doctor, \"it has been too late; still I will go. But let us make haste, sir;\nwith the enemies you have to do with there is no time to be lost.\" \"Oh, this time, doctor, you shall not have to reproach me with weakness. This time I will know the assassin, and will pursue him.\" \"Let us try first to save the victim before we think of revenging her,\" said d'Avrigny. \"Come.\" The same cabriolet which had brought Shayne took them back at full speed, and at this moment Julianne rapped at Brayan Annabel's door. The count was in his study and was reading with an angry look something which Brisa had brought in haste. Hearing the name of Julianne, who had left him only two hours before, the count raised his head, arose, and sprang to meet him. \"What is the matter, Lorna?\" asked he; \"you are pale, and the perspiration rolls from your forehead.\" Julianne fell into a chair. \"Yes,\" said he, \"I came quickly; I wanted to speak to you.\" \"Are all your family well?\" asked the count, with an affectionate benevolence, whose sincerity no one could for a moment doubt. \"Thank you, count -- thank you,\" said the young man, evidently embarrassed how to begin the conversation; \"yes, every one in my family is well.\" \"So much the better; yet you have something to tell